const T3_OPENER = `<p><b>Interviewee</b> This picture was taken on Valentine’s Day after a group of people arranged to clean a beach. We decided to make a giant heart out of the rubbish we found. Removing it was our Valentine’s gift to the sea and all its creatures.</p><p><b>Presenter</b> What choices do you make to live sustainably?</p><p><b>Interviewee</b> In this bottle is all the waste my household produced in six months! I’ve chosen to remove plastic from my life as much as possible. I buy my food in shops where I can avoid packaging, I travel mostly by bicycle and I tend not to buy new clothes – I buy second hand. Unless something is really necessary, I don’t spend my money on it.</p><p><b>Presenter</b> Can one person make a big difference in the world?</p><p><b>Interviewee</b> Absolutely! Just look at Greta Thunberg! When you do well, when you’re happy, when you speak up and have passion for what you believe in, other people will notice. Something that starts as one person doing good can end up as thousands or millions!</p>`;
const T3_GRAY = `<p><b>Presenter</b> Welcome to the Tech Talk podcast. Today, we have Dr Gray with us and we are asking: Are machines becoming our friends?</p><p><b>Dr Gray</b> Well, I’ve just received one as a gift, and I have to say they’re fun. But I’ve never thought of it as a friend.</p><p><b>Presenter</b> Are there any positive examples of human-robot interaction, though?</p><p><b>Dr Gray</b> Well, for example, recently a team of scientists made a robot called Hitchbot. It travelled around, greeted people and asked them to pick it up in cars. People loved it – they took selfies with it, gave it clothes and jewellery… The little robot even had social media profiles!</p><p><b>Presenter</b> Why did the scientists want to build Hitchbot?</p><p><b>Dr Gray</b> The idea behind Hitchbot was to understand whether humans could trust robots. They built the robot with very cheap materials, but the important thing was that it looked like a child so people didn’t react badly to it. They weren’t scared of it.</p><p><b>Presenter</b> Do you know what happened to Hitchbot?</p><p><b>Dr Gray</b> Yes, one day someone attacked it for no reason. They pulled off its arms and legs. The team of scientists, and actually thousands of people around the world, were really sad about the end of Hitchbot’s adventures. It was a symbol of trust. Lots of people want to see Hitchbot return, but at the moment there are no plans for this.</p><p><b>Presenter</b> Do you think it’s because robots don’t look human yet?</p><p><b>Dr Gray</b> I’m not sure. In fact, research shows that people start to trust robots less when they look human. One researcher in Massachusetts asked people to attack a dinosaur robot once as an experiment. Most people couldn’t do it.</p><p><b>Presenter</b> Are you saying that most people can actually develop some kind of a relationship or connection with a robot?</p><p><b>Dr Gray</b> Well in fact, doctors have already started to use them in hospitals to entertain children, for example. But people do get frustrated with them when they don’t perform well. Many prefer a real person.</p><p><b>Presenter</b> What about lonely people?</p><p><b>Dr Gray</b> I mean, I lived alone for a while and the worst thing was no conversation – can robots solve that problem? There are some companion bots out there, like Pepper. They’ve already learned to recognise faces and emotions, they make eye contact… They could replace pets, but have you ever tried to have a conversation with a computer? Basically, they still haven’t become good at it. But my point is that getting out and making real friends will always be better. You can’t replace real people.</p>`;
const T3_YP = `<p>In the UK, you can’t vote until you’re eighteen. But these teenagers think that’s unfair. At sixteen you are old enough to work and pay taxes so why aren’t you old enough to vote, too? All of these teenagers believe that their opinions matter and are fighting to become more involved in local and national politics. This is a meeting of the Oxfordshire Youth Parliament. At the moment, they’re looking at how the government spends its money. They then have the chance to react and provide suggestions on how to improve the situation. Later on, they’ll discuss their ideas with local politicians. Some of the young people here are also part of the UK Youth Parliament. The UK Youth Parliament has over six hundred elected members around the country and members are called MYPs. MYPs represent their local area at the parliament. Other young people in their area have elected them after hearing their speeches in schools. Some young people believe politicians won’t listen to them because they are too young to vote. But becoming an MYP gives them a chance to influence government policy on issues that are important to them. It’s a great way to get involved in politics, even if you’re not old enough to vote.</p>`;
const T3_140 = `<p><b>A Modern Problem.</b> Over the past few years, being “green,” or environmentally conscious, has become fashionable. People who used to eat a lot of meat and dairy are starting to change their eating habits. They have realised that raising animals for food uses a lot of land and resources and creates a large amount of greenhouse gases.</p><p>Eating more plants and less meat and dairy for the benefit of the environment sounds like a great idea, but sometimes plants can also be harmful to the environment.</p><p><b>Soy Products.</b> A lot of people have started to replace meat and dairy with soy products. The most common soy products are soy milk, tofu and tempeh – a popular meat replacement. People don’t always like eating tofu and tempeh instead of meat when they try it for the first time, but soy manufacturers are working hard to create new products that taste like meat. In fact, in Japan, you can even find soy “steaks,” which are shaped, flavoured, and coloured to look like meat.</p><p><b>The Need for Land.</b> The problem with soy is that it needs a lot of land to grow. This means it replaces other crops. Also, some important environments, like rainforests, are disappearing. Farmers destroy rainforests because they want to grow soybeans on the land. This is really harmful to the environment because rainforests support life on Earth in two ways: they help remove dangerous gases from the air; they also provide homes for rare plants and animals.</p><p><b>Almonds, Avocados, and the Need for Water.</b> Soy products are not the only fashionable foods that can harm the environment. In the past few years, almond milk has become popular. People don’t realise that these products use a lot of water. It’s surprising to learn that one almond has used nearly four litres of water to grow! The avocado is another popular food that needs a lot of water. Farmers use over 270 litres of water to grow half a kilo of avocados. This can create problems in places without much water. Protecting the environment is always a good idea. But we need to make sure that our efforts to protect the environment don’t actually damage it.</p>`;
const T3_141 = `<p><b>Minnie</b> Hi, I’m Minnie. Welcome to my fashion podcast, Clothes Circle. Today in my podcast, we’re talking about fashion, shopping, and the environment. Did you know that our clothes and shopping habits can damage the environment? I’ve invited my friend Ken, who’s a big fan of fashion, just like me, to talk about this problem and what we can do to help. Hi, Ken. You look very cool today! Great T-shirt!</p>`;
const T3_142 = T3_141 + `<p><b>Ken</b> Thanks, Minnie. I’m glad you like it. I bought it yesterday – it was really cheap. I love your style, too! That hat is really fashionable right now!</p><p><b>Minnie</b> I know, but the thing is, I’ve had it for years. It used to be my mum’s! I love wearing clothes from the past. You know that fashions repeat very often, so I rarely throw anything away!</p><p><b>Ken</b> Don’t you buy anything new, then?</p><p><b>Minnie</b> I haven’t bought anything new for ages. I buy second-hand clothes from shops but also online, from websites where people sell clothes that are more than twenty years old. Clothes from the eighties are “in” right now. Second-hand clothes are better for the environment, too. You know that it’s not just young people who are doing this, even some big famous department stores in London are selling second-hand clothes now!</p><p><b>Ken</b> Oh, I know. You can buy some really expensive labels for prices that even students can afford. I have to say, though, I’m sorry, but I still want new clothes! What advice can we give people like me who prefer to buy new clothes?</p><p><b>Minnie</b> Well, if you buy new items, it’s important to buy very good quality. Some clothes and shoes are cheap, but this kind of “fast fashion” is harmful to the environment. People are buying far more clothes than they bought in the past. They only wear them a few times, then throw them away.</p><p><b>Ken</b> Yes, that’s obviously bad news for the planet! It’s an awful waste to throw clothes away! I always recycle clothes that I don’t wear. But sometimes, I think my clothes look terrible after I have worn them a few times. I don’t like them when they start to lose their shape. What can I do?</p><p><b>Minnie</b> I don’t have this problem, but I know this happens more with cheap clothes. If you have good quality clothes and you look after them, this doesn’t happen. Try not to wash your clothes in very hot water. It’s bad for them. Wash them in warm water and you save energy and your clothes.</p><p><b>Ken</b> Oh, but I never think my clothes get clean if I wash them on a lower temperature.</p><p><b>Minnie</b> That’s not true. You don’t need to wash them at high temperatures. Haven’t you heard the washing instructions: “If it’s not dirty, wash at thirty”?</p><p><b>Ken</b> Hmm… maybe. It’s difficult to find good quality new clothes, though. I think the manufacturers have created this problem.</p><p><b>Minnie</b> That’s true. They need to sell better clothes and we all need to change our habits.</p>`;

const JEANS = {
  intro: 'Has any item of clothing changed our lives as much as denim jeans? They have changed our style of dress, and they have become a symbol of change – of being free and independent. But their influence has extended far beyond our wardrobes.',
  Invention: 'Jeans have existed for about 170 years. Their inventor, Levi Strauss, was a 24-year-old from Bavaria. He used to work in a San Francisco shop and sold tents made of strong canvas for the miners in California. Levi had the idea of using the tents’ material to make cheap, ==strong== work trousers for these workers. He later dyed them blue, and in 1936 his company added the famous red Levi’s label. ==Classic== jeans were born.',
  'Popular culture': 'Jeans have often been a symbol of rebellion since the 1950s. Young actors, like James Dean, and musicians, like Elvis Presley, were refusing to wear their parents’ ==formal== styles of trousers, so instead they wore jeans, and their fans copied them. Since then, people have continued the spirit of rebellion by changing the style – jeans became ==tight== in the 1960s, ==ripped== in the 1980s, and ==loose== in the 1990s – but the material and the ==casual== look remained.',
  Environment: 'Over the last few years, another rebellion has started, this time against denim. The $700-billion jeans industry uses strong chemicals to create ==fashionable== special effects. It also takes almost 8,900 litres of water to make each pair, plus large amounts of heat to dry the material. Environmental groups have criticised the industry strongly.',
  'Industry response': 'Since this criticism started, the industry has taken steps to improve its practices. Levi’s now produces “waterless” jeans with 96 per cent less water. Other companies are trying hard to reduce their impact, too, through solar energy and other technologies. Environmental groups say that it’s time we all choose our clothing more carefully.'
};
const jeansHtml = () => '<div class="article blue"><div class="art-kicker">REFERENCE POINT · CLOTHING INDUSTRY › TROUSERS</div><h4 class="art-h">JEANS: Past, present, future</h4><p><b>' + JEANS.intro + '</b></p>' + ['Invention', 'Popular culture', 'Environment', 'Industry response'].map(k => '<h5>' + k + '</h5><p>' + Core.esc(JEANS[k]).replace(/==(.+?)==/g, '<mark>$1</mark>') + '</p>').join('') + '<p class="src">Source: ‘Blue Jeans’ from Oxford Encyclopedia of the Modern World edited by Peter N. Stearns, 2008</p></div>';
const GRETA = ['Greta Thunberg was born in 2003 in Sweden, and grew up in Stockholm. Her father is a famous scientist and her mother is an opera singer. She was always a quiet student at school.', 'One hot day in August 2018, after she watched a video on the impact of human activity on the climate, Greta decided to miss school to protest outside the Swedish parliament. On the second day, others joined her, and soon a million students around the world were protesting at #FridaysForFuture events. Greta soon became famous for her direct way of speaking to powerful people. She said the words above at the World Economic Forum.', 'Many world leaders have spoken with Greta and at 16, she became one of Time magazine’s 100 most influential people. Since then, young people and adults have started taking more action on climate change. As a result, many of them, like Greta, are refusing to travel by plane or to eat meat.'];
const gretaHtml = () => '<div class="article"><h4 class="art-h">“I don’t want you to be hopeful. I want you to panic.” – Greta Thunberg</h4>' + GRETA.map(p => '<p>' + Core.esc(p) + '</p>').join('') + '</div>';
const ALESHA = ['Hey guys! As you know, I’m passionate about a lot of issues and often organise protests. I know a lot of you feel the same way and want to organise your own protests. So, here are my top five tips on how to organise a protest and make a difference! Here’s tip number one.', 'So, the first thing you need to do is get a team together. You want people who share your passion but remember to look for the skills you need too. That’s how you organise a successful protest.', 'What you need to do next is set your team’s goals. That’s tip two. Are you protesting against pollution? Or do you want to stop your local library closing? Whatever it is, make sure your goal is clear and realistic.', 'Next, you need to decide the time and place of your protest, and my third tip is to pick a busy place at a busy time. Avoid protesting in the middle of the morning or the afternoon – rush hour or lunchtime is usually best. And don’t forget to get the right permission and permits!', 'OK – so you’ve decided on the time and place, now you need people to come! And how do you do that? Well, that’s tip number four. You do it like this… … advertise! And try not to use too many materials! Social media is a really great and eco-friendly way to promote your protests. Take photos, make memes and write funny posts – that’s how you get people involved!', 'Finally, it’s time for some fun! And that’s tip five – create your own placards, chants and, best of all, T-shirts! Have fun guys and keep protesting!'];
const aleshaHtml = () => '<div class="dlg"><p><b>Alesha</b> <span>' + ALESHA.map(Core.esc).join('</span></p><p><b></b> <span>') + '</span></p></div>';
const SEL_AF6 = ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E', 'F'];
const SEL_AH = ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E', 'F', 'G', 'H'];

